WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.138 --> 00:00:05.684
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.684 --> 00:00:09.050
I grew up being
a self-centered kid in school.

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:17.286
Sure, I could do fast math, but I did not
give a shit about others who could not.

00:00:17.286 --> 00:00:22.934
I could calculate the cube root of
738 in my ahead when I was just 12.

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:28.222
When my friends struggling
with basic fractions and

00:00:28.222 --> 00:00:31.925
decimals asked me for
help, I turned a blind eye.

00:00:31.925 --> 00:00:36.927
I hadn't mastered calculus yet,
the National Math Olympiad was around

00:00:36.927 --> 00:00:41.690
the corner, and my parents and
teachers had big expectations of me.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:45.207
I needed to focus on myself, and

00:00:45.207 --> 00:00:49.362
my friends would have done the same
thing if they were in my place.

00:00:53.706 --> 00:00:57.780
I received straight A's in the math and
sciences but

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:02.980
if there had been a class for
helping people, I would have got an F.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:09.851
The national education system in India,
like in many parts of the world

00:01:09.851 --> 00:01:14.701
is predicated on the idea of relative
performance and competition, and

00:01:14.701 --> 00:01:17.180
this dominates our view of success.

00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:24.144
I didn't think it was possible to break
out of this belief until I met two

00:01:24.144 --> 00:01:29.220
people four years back, Anju and
Xabi, two kids nearly half my age.

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:35.900
Anju just 15 years old lived in a village
that did not send girls to school.

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:41.149
But rather than try to move to a different
village and get her education,

00:01:41.149 --> 00:01:44.960
she devoted her energy in changing
the minds of people in our community.

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:48.929
Her determination changed the system, and

00:01:48.929 --> 00:01:53.520
she has now had more than 200 girls to
enroll in school and become educated.

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:56.386
Xabi just 16,

00:01:56.386 --> 00:02:02.400
convinced his school principal to start
an animal shelter in their school campus.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:07.350
While he, and his fellow
classmates have rescued more than

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:09.829
300 injured dogs to date.

00:02:09.829 --> 00:02:14.915
I was fascinated with Anju and Xabi,
for the first time in my life I met

00:02:14.915 --> 00:02:20.990
teenagers who cared deeply about others,
and took action to make things better.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:27.522
I certainly wasn't like that when I was in
school and neither were the kids I knew.

00:02:29.794 --> 00:02:30.960
So, why was that?

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:36.965
Why were Anju and Xabi the outliers and
not the mainstream?

00:02:36.965 --> 00:02:42.185
The more I thought about it, it all
pointed to our education systems and

00:02:42.185 --> 00:02:46.190
the extreme competitive
pressure that is put on kids.

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:51.680
You got good grades,
great now get better grades.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:55.180
You made it into a good college,
fine, now get into a better college.

00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:59.443
Life becomes this competitive race and

00:02:59.443 --> 00:03:04.274
this race engulfs children in
an inward looking me focus mindset

00:03:04.274 --> 00:03:08.940
rather than developing an outward
looking we focus mindset.

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:14.942
Children leave schools with impressive
grades and certificates and

00:03:14.942 --> 00:03:19.074
get decent jobs, but they miss
becoming empathetic, responsible,

00:03:19.074 --> 00:03:21.185
conscious citizens of this world.

00:03:23.904 --> 00:03:29.569
But should school systems choose between
academic excellence and empathy and

00:03:29.569 --> 00:03:34.560
compassion, I didn't think so and
I chose to do something about it.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:39.640
I quit my job and started a social
enterprise to try and tackle this issue.

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:45.816
As part of our research, I read a lot
about people who have dedicated their

00:03:45.816 --> 00:03:50.570
lives towards the betterment of society,
and how they develop their passion for it.

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:56.570
That's when I was introduced to
Bill Drayton, Bill is credited by many for

00:03:56.570 --> 00:04:00.610
coining the term social
entrepreneur back in the 1970s.

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:05.973
Ashoka the nonprofit he founded support
social entrepreneurs in more than 40

00:04:05.973 --> 00:04:06.990
countries today.

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:13.130
But what's interesting is this, when Bill
was 11 If he had started an initiative

00:04:13.130 --> 00:04:17.360
to help his classmates by
creating a class newspaper.

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:20.554
He skipped a lot of classes because
of his strong commitment and

00:04:20.554 --> 00:04:22.310
wasn't even home most evenings.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:26.608
When his mother died,
he found correspondence between her and

00:04:26.608 --> 00:04:28.410
a school principal.

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:32.968
His mother was concerned she asked, why
is a fifth grader not in your school or

00:04:32.968 --> 00:04:33.541
at home?

00:04:35.066 --> 00:04:39.382
The school principal wrote back saying,
trust your son, and

00:04:39.382 --> 00:04:42.040
please don't show you're anxious.

00:04:43.608 --> 00:04:47.972
But he was unaware of this at the time,
but when he thinks back on it,

00:04:47.972 --> 00:04:53.270
he says bless principle who encouraged my
ability to make an impact in this world.

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:59.014
We found two patterns in the stories
of people like Bill, number one,

00:04:59.014 --> 00:05:03.641
most develop their passion for creating
change by going through an outward

00:05:03.641 --> 00:05:07.029
centric change making
experience at a very young age.

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:11.572
And number two, they had at
least one adult who believed and

00:05:11.572 --> 00:05:13.470
supported them in their journey.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:18.382
Inspired by these learnings, our team
designed a program for middle school and

00:05:18.382 --> 00:05:20.210
high school children.

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:25.087
Our idea was to encourage the kids to
identify social or environmental issues in

00:05:25.087 --> 00:05:29.670
their home, school or community,
support them as it took action to try and

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:33.420
tackle these issues and
appreciate them for their efforts.

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:39.499
Our role as facilitators was to do
three simple things, encourage,

00:05:39.499 --> 00:05:44.090
support and appreciate, or
what we call the ESA framework in short.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:49.090
Who could be against such a program,
right?

00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:52.840
Yet we couldn't convince a single
school in the first two months.

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:56.598
Schools rejected us outright saying
that they did not see any value and

00:05:56.598 --> 00:05:59.730
that children needed to focus
solely on their academics.

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:04.011
We finally offered the entire
program to schools for free for

00:06:04.011 --> 00:06:06.740
an entire year to get them to try it out.

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:10.788
Not an ideal start for us but
we took what we could get.

00:06:10.788 --> 00:06:15.576
If convincing schools and
parents was tricky, thankfully working

00:06:15.576 --> 00:06:21.090
with children was the exact opposite,
children loved our program.

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:24.258
They quickly identified the problems
they wanted to tackle and

00:06:24.258 --> 00:06:25.640
passionately got to work.

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:32.322
Chandan just 11 years old told us
I want everyone in my school to be

00:06:32.322 --> 00:06:33.630
physically fit.

00:06:34.730 --> 00:06:39.329
His school did not offer a physical
fitness program, so he gathered a group of

00:06:39.329 --> 00:06:43.600
other sixth graders and started
a student run yoga class for everyone.

00:06:44.950 --> 00:06:49.684
Even two years since our program ended,
the children still run their yoga class

00:06:49.684 --> 00:06:54.570
and in fact have expanded their
initiative to five nearby schools.

00:06:54.570 --> 00:06:59.533
Incredible, Jonathan's teacher
recently told us that Chandan was

00:06:59.533 --> 00:07:01.764
an extremely shy kid at first.

00:07:01.764 --> 00:07:06.279
But once he got a platform to
solve a problem he cared about,

00:07:06.279 --> 00:07:12.058
he discovered a new side of himself,
Chandan is no longer that timid child,

00:07:12.058 --> 00:07:14.878
he's an empowered agent of change.

00:07:14.878 --> 00:07:19.938
Kielty just 15 started the Code Red
initiative to spread awareness about

00:07:19.938 --> 00:07:24.690
menstrual hygiene in underprivileged
communities near her school.

00:07:25.790 --> 00:07:29.488
She was so successful that now
three years since her program,

00:07:29.488 --> 00:07:33.269
she's helping an NGO create
awareness about cerebral palsy.

00:07:35.335 --> 00:07:38.055
Told me in my 10 years of schooling,

00:07:38.055 --> 00:07:44.300
I never had an adult who encouraged me
to think about helping my community.

00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:48.133
But as soon as I received that
encouragement, my life changed and

00:07:48.133 --> 00:07:52.399
now I want to continue tackling meaningful
issues for the rest of my life.

00:07:53.830 --> 00:07:56.680
This was common amongst all the students.

00:07:58.430 --> 00:08:02.337
After working with more than
25,000 children over three years,

00:08:02.337 --> 00:08:04.300
our biggest realization was this.

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:08.760
All children are born with empathy and

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:13.740
compassion, the challenge is to
remain with it as we grow up.

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:20.379
These humane values can either thrive and
flourish with encouragement and

00:08:20.379 --> 00:08:25.113
support from adults or they can be
completely stifled and suppressed.

00:08:25.113 --> 00:08:27.550
Which is what happens through
our education systems.

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:34.192
Which one do we all want for
our children, for our community?

00:08:34.192 --> 00:08:38.966
We have big problems today
political polarization,

00:08:38.966 --> 00:08:42.574
growing inequality, climate change,

00:08:42.574 --> 00:08:48.528
we must fundamentally change the way
we are raising our children.

00:08:48.528 --> 00:08:52.586
We must set them free from this
inward looking me focused mindset,

00:08:52.586 --> 00:08:55.203
to an outward looking we focused mindset.

00:08:56.574 --> 00:09:00.559
Looking back, I don't believe my career
would have been any less successful if I

00:09:00.559 --> 00:09:02.740
had more empathy and
compassion as a child.

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:04.734
But I can't help but

00:09:04.734 --> 00:09:08.350
wonder how much better the lives of my
other young friends would have been?

00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:13.388
Would their careers be better, would they
have passed the along the empathy they had

00:09:13.388 --> 00:09:15.230
experienced onto their own children?

00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:21.215
Academicians, researchers and advocates
are fighting to include empathy and

00:09:21.215 --> 00:09:24.600
compassion into the formal curriculum
at the institutional level.

00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:29.889
And each one of us, you and me, can make
a difference at the individual level.

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:38.240
Implement ESA, encourage the children in
your life to think about and help others.

00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:42.375
Support them when they
start something meaningful,

00:09:42.375 --> 00:09:45.300
and appreciate them for
when they put in the efforts.

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:50.151
Be their champion,
the one who keeps their empathy alive so

00:09:50.151 --> 00:09:53.000
that no matter who they
become professionally or

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:57.710
where they go in the future,
they create a better world for everyone.

00:09:57.710 --> 00:09:59.241
Thank you very much.

00:09:59.241 --> 00:10:02.749
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:10:02.749 --> 00:10:05.183
[MUSIC]

